lement-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stone</surname><given-names>S. P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Halligan</surname><given-names>P. W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenwood</surname><given-names>R. J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The incidence of neglect phenomena and related disorders in patients with an acute right or left hemisphere stroke.</article-title><source>Age and Ageing</source><year>(1993)</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>46</fpage><lpage>52</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8438666</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R28"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vallar</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ronchi</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Anosognosia for motor and sensory deficits after unilateral brain damage: A review.</article-title><source>Restorative Neurology and Neuroscience</source><year>(2006)</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>247</fpage><lpage>257</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17119302</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R29"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vuilleumier</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Anosognosia: The neurology of beliefs and uncertainties.</article-title><source>Cortex</source><year>(2004)</year><volume>40</volume><fpage>9</fpage><lpage>17</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15070000</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R30"><element-citation publication-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wade</surname><given-names>D. T.</given-names></name></person-group><source>Measurement in neurological rehabilitation</source><year>(1992)</year><publisher-loc>Oxford, UK</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Medical Publications</publisher-name></element-citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="FN1"><p><sup>1</sup> Note that the degree of unawareness strictly depends on the degree of motor impairment. For example, a patient with mild motor impairment would receive quite a low score from the caregiver (e.g., 10). If the patient is completely unaware of this mild motor disorder, the discrepancy score would be no higher than 10, i.e., into the range of mild unawareness. According to this analysis, unawareness of a mild deficit would never indicate a severe pathological phenomenon.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>